44ae426082}}\f0\fs20\expnd8\expndtw40\charscalex100  \f5\fs22\i1\expnd0\expndtw0 Boundary edges\f6\i0 . These are edges which form, as the name suggests, the boundary of the surface. A boundary edge is characterized by the fact that it has \f4\b1 always \f6\b0 only \f4\b1{\ul 1} \f6\b0 face attached to it. Examples of boundary-edges are the sheer line (when the ship is not fitted with a deck) or the centerline of the ship. The centerline, or profile, is in fact a special case. When defining the hull only its portside is created. So all edges on the center plane are boundary-edges as\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 they have\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 only\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 one face connected to it.\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 In reality\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 the ship\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 symmetric, and when performing calculations FREE!ship creates a virtual symmetric ship by mirroring the model in the center plane.\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery852\footery852\margtsxn1300\margbsxn1040\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li1003\fi-350\ri419\sl235\slmult1\sb203\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100{\*\shppict{\pict
