44ae426082}}\f0\fs20\expnd8\expndtw40\charscalex100  \f5\fs22\i1\expnd0\expndtw0 Regular edges\f6\i0 . These are all other edges away from the boundary of the surface, and must \f4\b1 always \f6\b0 be shared by\expnd0\expndtw-2  \f4\b1\expnd0\expndtw0{\ul 2\expnd0\expndtw-2}\expnd0\expndtw-2  \f6\b0\expnd0\expndtw0 adjacent faces. Regular edges are drawn as\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 dark-gray\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 lines.\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 The two faces connected to an edge are joined smoothly along their shared edge. It is possible however to mark an edge as a crease-edge. When doing so, the two faces are joined in a tangent-discontinuous way. In other words, crease-edges are used to define knuckle lines. A boundary-edge is in fact a specific case of a crease edge since there is no second face to make a smooth transition.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri521\sl235\slmult1\sb251\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 FREE!ship uses the presence of boundary-edges in its calculations. By doing so it is able to determine when the ship is making water when for example the deck line is submerged. The downside to this\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 that it is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 critical\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 for any\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 regular edge to be connected to two\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 faces, at least when not submerged. Having two different edges which are located precisely on top of each other is not sufficient. Faces have to be physically connected to the \f4\b1 same \f6\b0 edge. There is also another reason for this which will be explained in {\field {\fldinst { HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark3" }}{\fldrslt 1.6 Guidelines to subdivision modeling}}
. Boundary-edges from which both the startpoint and endpoint are located on the center plane are excluded from this test. In reality\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 these edges are connected to both the portside and\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 the starboard side of the ship and are therefore not truly a boundary-edge.\par\pard\plain\s3\ls15\ilvl1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li714\fi-430\qj\sl240\slmult1\sb241\tx714{\*\bkmkstart 1.4 Points.}{\*\bkmkend 1.4 Points.}\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100\f4\fs26\b1\expnd0\expndtw-2 Points.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri496\qj\sl235\slmult1\sb59\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Points form the basis of the surface. Most\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of the editing is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 done\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 on a\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 point-level\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 by\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 moving points to different locations, inserting new\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 points or\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 removing existing points. Basically\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 there are two different types of points of interest to the user. These are:\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb7\f6\fs22\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li1003\fi-350\ri757\qj\sl235\slmult1\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100{\*\shppict{\pict
