\par}}}}\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\sl235\slmult1\sb1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 row of points have to be inserted as demonstrated in the figure to the right. This results in having more controlpoints than actually needed or desired, and more controlpoints mean more work to the designer.\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Also\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 very\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 complex\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 shapes\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 cannot\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 be\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 modeled\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 using\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 only one surface. When using multiple surfaces the designer is challenged with the difficult task of aligning these surfaces at their boundaries. It is often desirable to maintain a smooth transition along these boundaries. Each time one of these surfaces is modified, the other surface has to be modified by the user to maintain this smooth transition.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1 \column \f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb174\f6\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li277\sl240\slmult1\f5\fs20\b0\i1\expnd0\expndtw-2\charscalex100 Illustration\expnd2\expndtw10  \expnd-2\expndtw-10 3\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery852\footery852\margtsxn1300\margbsxn1040\cols1\colno1\colw10484\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb180\f5\fs22\b0\i1\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri556\sl235\slmult1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 To overcome these problems FREE!ship makes use of subdivision\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 surfaces. Subdivision\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 surfaces also use controlpoints as a modeling handle, just like NURBS or B-Splines. With subdivision surfaces the grid of controlpoints does not need to be rectangular, but calculating a point on the surface is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 more difficult since\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the surface is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 not parametric. Instead the controlmesh is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 refined and smoothed in a number of steps. Each step is called a \'93subdivision step\'94, hence the name subdivision surfaces. This process is clarified in {\field {\fldinst { HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark1" }}{\fldrslt Illustration 4}}
