\par}}}}\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri556\sl235\slmult1\sb61\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 When a new\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 model is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 opened\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 or started the program by\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 default adds\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 4 windows.\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Each window\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 has a different view on the 3D hull. The area of the window on which the model is drawn is called a viewport. Zooming in or out on the viewport is done by\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri4785\sl235\slmult1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 pressing\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 left mouse\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 button\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 and\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 moving\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 mouse up or down while keeping the left button pressed.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri4849\sl235\slmult1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Users having a mouse wheel may find it convenient to zoom in or out using their mouse wheel.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Panning the viewport\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 done\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 in\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 similar\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 manner, only\expnd-1\expndtw-7  \expnd0\expndtw0 with\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 right mouse button must be pressed. Only when the viewport displays a perspective view, as in the image to the right, two scrollbars are visible. These can be used to rotate and tilt the model. Another way\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 to rotate the model is keeping the middle mouse button (or mouse wheel) pressed while dragging the mouse, also\par\sect\sectd\sbknone\cols2\colno1\colw5430\colsr150\colno2\colw4904\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\sl-236\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 only\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 in\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 in\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 perspective\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 view.\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 Additional\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 options\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd-1\expndtw-5 for\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li283\sl-178\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 \column \f5\fs20\i1\expnd0\expndtw-2 Illustration\expnd2\expndtw10  \expnd-2\expndtw-10 8\par\sect\sectd\sbknone\cols1\colno1\colw10484\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri419\sl235\slmult1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 each\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 viewport\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 are accessible from the popup-menu which\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 shows\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 after the right button of the mouse has been pressed.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb228\f6\fs22\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s3\ls16\ilvl1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li714\fi-430\sl240\slmult1\tx714{\*\bkmkstart 2.2 Selecting items.}{\*\bkmkend 2.2 Selecting items.}\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100\f4\fs26\b1 Selecting\expnd-2\expndtw-9  \expnd0\expndtw-2 items.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri440\sl235\slmult1\sb61\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Only items visible in the viewport can be selected and only if the viewp{\*\bkmkstart _bookmark5}{\*\bkmkend _bookmark5}ort is in wireframe mode (shading is turned off). In order to select points or edges the {\field {\fldinst { HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark5" }}{\fldrslt \cf3{\ul controlnet}}
}\cf3 {\ul } \cf1 has to be turned on. Faces can only be selected when the {\field {\fldinst { HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark4" }}{\fldrslt \cf3{\ul interior edges}}
}\cf3 {\ul } {\*\bkmkstart _bookmark4}{\*\bkmkend _bookmark4}\expnd1\expndtw4\expnd0\expndtw0\cf1 are turned on. For more information concerning visibility options the reader is referred to {\field {\fldinst { HYPERLINK \\l "bookmark38" }}{\fldrslt 11. Visibility options}}
\cf2 . \cf1 It is important to keep in mind that selected faces, edges or points \f4\b1 remain \f6\b0 selected even when they are not visible in the viewport due to a change in the visibility options. To select an item, simply click on it with the mouse. Selected items can be recognized because they\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 are drawn\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 in\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 yellow.\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 If a\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 point\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 selected, and\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 the user\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 clicks on a different point, this new point will be selected and the previous point will be deselected.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri569\sl232\slmult1\sb5\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Selecting multiple\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 points however\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 possible\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 by\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 keeping the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 CTRL-key\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 pressed\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 while\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 clicking\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 on\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 a new point.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri440\sl235\slmult1\sb1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 If the CTRL key is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 pressed while\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 an edge is being selected, the\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 program tries to trace the edge until a irregular point is encountered or an edge with a different crease-property. This way it is easy to select an entire row of edges (edgeloop) such as for example the sheerline or a hard chine.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri771\sl235\slmult1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Faces also can be CTRL-selected. In that case all the faces belonging to the same layer and connected to the selected one are also selected or deselected. Faces that are isolated from the selected face because they are totally surrounded by crease edges are not included.\par\pard\plain\s3\ls16\ilvl1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li714\fi-430\sl240\slmult1\sb233\tx714{\*\bkmkstart 2.3 Dragging control points.}{\*\bkmkend 2.3 Dragging control points.}\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100\f4\fs26\b1 Dragging\expnd-1\expndtw-7  \expnd0\expndtw0 control\expnd-1\expndtw-6  \expnd0\expndtw-2 points.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri455\sl235\slmult1\sb60\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 One of the most important options when it comes to modeling the hull is dragging points. In order to do this the controlnet must be turned on. Although it is possible to select points in a perspective view, the actual dragging of the point can only be done in the bodyplan view, profile view or plan view. When dragging a controlpoint all information is updated realtime. This includes stations, buttocks, waterlines\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 and\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 diagonals. Especially\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 when\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 precision\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 of the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 model\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 set\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 to\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 high\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 value this updating might become slow since each intersection with the hull must be recalculated. If it becomes too slow, try using a lower precision. If it is still too slow, switch off some of these intersection curves since only visible items are recalculated, or try using less intersection curves.\par\sect\