\par}}}}\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb12\f5\fs20\b0\i1\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f5\fs22\b0\i1\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb34\f5\fs22\b0\i1\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri534\sl235\slmult1\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 The image above shows a controlcurve in the aft part of a container ship. To the left the controlcurve\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 is shown\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 in\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 blue,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 while\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 to\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 the right the controlcurve\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 show in\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 selected state (yellow) together with it's curvature plot (fuchsia). The straight parts of the\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 curve\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 have\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 zero\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 curvature. If you travel along the curve from the bottom to the direction of the deck, first the curve starts bending to the left. In this area the curvature is positive. At a height of about 2.5 meters the curve starts bending to the right, here the curvature becomes negative. A little bit further along the curve it bends\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 to the left again, so the curvature becomes positive. How\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 is this\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 translated into\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the curvature plot? At a number of points on the curve the curvature is calculated and drawn as a line, perpendicular to the curve. The larger the line, the larger the curvature. If the curvature is positive the line is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 drawn on the opposite site of the curve. So\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 while\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the absolute value\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of the curvature in a point\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 is not\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 that interesting, the way\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 it\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 changes along the curve\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 is. This is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 a measure of the fairness of the curve. You don't want abrupt changes in the curvature, it should vary as smoothly as\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery852\footery852\margtsxn1300\margbsxn1040\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li284\ri569\sl235\slmult1\sb187\f6\fs22\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 possible. And very often, especially with small boats and yachts, a change of the sign of the curvature as seen in the image above is highly undesirable. Below is an example of a controlcurve from a\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 sailing yacht. The upper part of the image shows\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 poorly\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 faired curve. We see a\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 change of the curvature sign in an area where it should be positive, followed shortly after that by a sudden increase in the size of the curvature. After that the curvature size rapidly drops, just to increase again at the bow. The lower half of the image shows the same controlcurve after being faired. It is obvious that the curvature changes gradually now and the curve is very smooth.\par
